{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"23b10cd1-0e9a-4d85-a44d-de89504a131b","step":{"id":"s1","script":"This cubic has two turning points. A local maximum is higher than nearby points; a local minimum is lower than nearby points.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Local extrema of a cubic"},{"op":"equation","id":"function","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"f(x)=x^3-3x"},{"op":"figure","id":"graph","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-2.2,2.2],"y":[-4.5,4.5],"axes":"edge","x_label":"x","y_label":"f(x)"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"cubic","y":"x^3-3*x","domain":[-2.1,2.1]}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"23b10cd1-0e9a-4d85-a44d-de89504a131b","step":{"id":"s2","script":"At each smooth turning point, the tangent is horizontal. Set the derivative equal to zero to find the candidates.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"derivative","anchor":{"el":"function","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"f'(x)=3x^2-3=0\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x=\\pm1"},{"op":"figure","id":"candidates","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"maximum","at":{"on":"cubic","x":-1},"label":"(-1, 2)"},{"kind":"point","id":"minimum","at":{"on":"cubic","x":1},"label":"(1, -2)"},{"kind":"tangent","to":"cubic","x":-1,"length":0.25,"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"tangent","to":"cubic","x":1,"length":0.25,"stroke":"dashed"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.warning","request_id":"23b10cd1-0e9a-4d85-a44d-de89504a131b","code":"step_repaired","step_hint":"s3"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"23b10cd1-0e9a-4d85-a44d-de89504a131b","step":{"id":"s3","script":"The derivative is positive outside these two values and negative between them. Increasing then decreasing gives a maximum; decreasing then increasing gives a minimum.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"signs","anchor":{"el":"derivative","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\begin{array}{c|ccc}x&x<-1&-1<x<1&x>1\\\\ f'(x)&+&-&+\\end{array}"},{"op":"figure","id":"directions","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[-1.8,-2.8],"text":"↗","size":"large"},{"kind":"text","at":[0,1],"text":"↘","size":"large"},{"kind":"text","at":[1.8,2.8],"text":"↗","size":"large"}]}],"checkpoint":{"question":"Why is x = 1 a local minimum rather than a maximum?","expected_gist":"The derivative changes from negative to positive."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"23b10cd1-0e9a-4d85-a44d-de89504a131b","step":{"id":"s4","script":"So the local maximum is two and the local minimum is negative two. These are nearby comparisons, not absolute bounds on the cubic.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"results","anchor":{"el":"signs","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\text{Local max: }f(-1)=2\\qquad\\text{Local min: }f(1)=-2"},{"op":"figure","id":"labels","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[-1,3.3],"text":"local max"},{"kind":"text","at":[1,-3.3],"text":"local min"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
